Here is a professional template for reporting a critical Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) vulnerability, followed by a guide on how to hunt for Subdomain Takeovers, which are high-value targets in bug bounty programs.
------------------------------
## Part 1: Writing a High-Value Critical SSRF Report
When you discover an SSRF that exposes internal cloud metadata (like AWS keys), you must document it perfectly. Platforms like YesWeHack or HackerOne will award a Critical (P1) severity payout only if you clearly demonstrate the business risk.
Use this structured format for your submission:

### Title
Critical SSRF on [://target.com] leading to AWS Cloud Infrastructure Takeover

### Vulnerability Description
A Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF) vulnerability exists in the image preview component of the application. The `url` parameter does not validate or restrict user-supplied inputs on the backend server. An attacker can manipulate this parameter to force the hosting infrastructure to make outbound HTTP requests to internal, non-public network interfaces.

### Vulnerable Endpoint
URL: https://://target.com.php?url=FUZZ
Parameter: url

### Proof of Concept (PoC) Steps
1. Navigate to the vulnerable application endpoint.
2. Inject the converted decimal payload for the AWS Cloud Metadata IP address into the `url` parameter:
   https://://target.com.php?url=http://2852039166/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/
3. Observe the HTTP response data returned in the browser layout. 
4. The backend bypasses standard string checks due to the decimal conversion, queries the internal cloud layer, and dumps active, unauthenticated AWS IAM role credential blocks.

### Impact
An attacker can extract active AWS Access Keys, Secret Keys, and Session Tokens. These credentials allow complete access to the company's internal cloud infrastructure, database snapshots, and private cloud storage buckets, completely compromising data confidentiality and integrity.

### Remediation Guidance
1. Implement a strict allowlist for the `url` parameter, accepting only trusted external image domains.
2. Disable internal metadata endpoints or enforce IMDSv2 (Instance Metadata Service Version 2) on AWS, which requires a session token header to prevent simple SSRF exploits.

