## Part 2: Hunting for Subdomain Takeovers
A Subdomain Takeover happens when a target company points a subdomain (like ://target.com) to a third-party service (like GitHub Pages, Shopify, or Zendesk) using a DNS CNAME record, but then deletes or cancels their account with that service.
If they forget to delete the DNS record, anyone can sign up for a free account on that service and claim the subdomain, allowing them to host a malicious site under the target company's official brand name.
## 1. Automated Subdomain Discovery
To hunt for takeovers, you must first gather a massive list of subdomains and inspect their DNS CNAME records.

# Install the required subfinder and dnsx tools via Go
go install -v ://github.com
go install -v ://github.com
# 1. Gather all subdomains for your target company
~/go/bin/subfinder -d target.com -o subdomains.txt
# 2. Check the CNAME pointers of those subdomains to find third-party hosts
~/go/bin/dnsx -l subdomains.txt -cname -resp | grep "CNAME" > cnames.txt

## 2. Spotting the Vulnerability Fingerprint
Open your cnames.txt file and look at where the subdomains point. If a subdomain points to a third-party service, look for these common "Dangling DNS" fingerprints when you visit the link in your browser:

| Third-Party Service | DNS CNAME Pointer | Error Message Fingerprint (Vulnerable!) |
|---|---|---|
| GitHub Pages | username.github.io | "There isn't a GitHub Pages site here." |
| Shopify | ://myshopify.com | "Sorry, this shop is currently unavailable." |
| Amazon S3 | ://amazonaws.com | "The specified bucket does not exist." |
| Zendesk | ://zendesk.com | "No such app" or "Help Center Closed" |

## 3. Executing the Takeover
If you find a subdomain like ://target.com pointing to Zendesk, and it throws a "No such app" error:

   1. Go to Zendesk.com and sign up for a free trial account.
   2. During setup, when it asks for your desired workspace name, type the exact sub-name used by the company (e.g., target).
   3. Once claimed, your trial dashboard will completely control ://target.com. You can now host phishing pages or steal session cookies from the main target.com domain.

